= The ban

On 23 November General Seeckt, who holds executive power, bans the KPD. The ban lasts until 1 March 1924. The party's papers are shut, its offices closed and its leaders hunted. The NSDAP was banned on the same day, in the same decree, which tells you what the government thinks of the difference between its enemies.

The party has to choose how to survive.

- @underground: Go underground, with secret cells and illegal papers.
- @legal: Fight the ban in the courts and keep to legal forms.

@underground
subtitle: dev: kpd_banned = 1, kpd_ban_end = time + 3, kf_right_strength +5, resources −1, workers_kpd −1
on-arrival: kpd_banned = 1; kpd_ban_end = time + 3; kf_right_strength += 5; resources -= 1; workers_kpd -= 1
go-to: root

The party falls back on cells and false names. This is a world the party's organisation men, Pieck and Eberlein, know. The Comintern's agents, who have seen this before, are in their element, and the more the party depends on them the less it depends on its own members.

@legal
subtitle: dev: kpd_banned = 1, kpd_ban_end = time + 3, kf_right_strength +4, resources −1
on-arrival: kpd_banned = 1; kpd_ban_end = time + 3; kf_right_strength += 4; resources -= 1
go-to: root

We file complaints and send delegations to the courts. The judges are patient with us, as they were with the generals in 1920, and they decide nothing. A legalist party under a ban is a party that does very little.
